What this is, before anything else.

terminal-minceraft exists to demonstrate one thing: that a browser game can be
rendered into a terminal pane, and played there. That is the whole point of the
project, and it is the reason the game is here at all.

It is not a way to play Minecraft without paying for it. Minecraft is a
commercial product and playing it requires a licence from Mojang. Buy the game:
https://www.minecraft.net

The MIT licence in LICENSE covers the code written for this project. It does
not cover the whole of what ends up on your machine.

terminal-minceraft is three things. Only the first of them is in this
repository. Read this before copying any of it.

1. The wrapper, MIT (see LICENSE)
   bin/terminal-minceraft, install.sh, web/serve.mjs, web/look.js, scripts/*
   Written for this project. This is everything in this repository.

2. EaglercraftX 1.8, compiled on your machine when you install, under its own terms
   web/client.html
   Eaglercraft is a browser port of Minecraft. It is not in this repository, it
   is not distributed by this project, and no part of it is redistributed here.
   There is no prebuilt copy to hand out either: scripts/build-client.sh
   compiles web/client.html at install time, from inputs pinned to exact
   commits and sha256s:

     - lax1dude's eaglercraft-1_8 source tree. It holds the build tools, the
       browser runtime and patch files, and deliberately contains no Minecraft
       code: https://gitflic.ru/project/lax1dude/eaglercraft-1_8.git
     - the official Minecraft 1.8.8 client jar, downloaded from Mojang's own
       servers at install time
     - the Mod Coder Pack for 1.8.8, used to decompile that jar:
       https://github.com/leijurv/MineBot
     - TeaVM 0.9.2 from Maven Central, which compiles Java to JavaScript
     - EaglerForgeInjector (MIT), run over the result so --agent works:
       https://github.com/EaglerForge/EaglerForgeInjector

   The client that comes out contains decompiled Minecraft code. It is not
   ours, it is not MIT, and it is not ours to relicense or to redistribute.
   Whether you may have a copy of it is between you, its authors, and Mojang.
   Repositories hosting Eaglercraft have been taken down under the DMCA before,
   which is why nothing is shipped or mirrored here, and why --client lets you
   point it at a copy you already have.

3. Minecraft, a Mojang product
   Minecraft is a trademark of Mojang Synergies AB, part of Microsoft. This
